3735 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (So now if you are wondering, can we use a simple join in place of the above queries? Then the answer is NO, if you replace CROSS/OUTER APPLY in the above queries with INNER JOIN/LEFT OUTER JOIN, specify ON clause \(something as 1=1\) and run the query, you will get "The multi-part identifier "D.DepartmentID" could not be bound." error. This is because with JOINs the execution context of outer query is different from the execution context of the function \(or a derived table\), and you can not bind a value/variable from the outer query to the function as a parameter. Hence the APPLY operator is required for such queries.)
/E (So now if you are wondering, can we use a simple join in place of the above queries? Then the answer is NO, if you replace CROSS/OUTER APPLY in the above queries with INNER JOIN/LEFT OUTER JOIN, specify ON clause \(something as 1=1\) and run the query, you will get "The multi-part identifier "D.DepartmentID" could not be bound." error. This is because with JOINs the execution context of outer query is different from the execution context of the function \(or a derived table\), and you can not bind a value/variable from the outer query to the function as a parameter. Hence the APPLY operator is required for such queries.)
/Pg 58 0 R
/K [13]
>>
endobj
