/T (Chapter 39: NULL Introduction NULL in SQL, as well as programming in general, means literally "nothing". In SQL, it is easier to understand as "the absence of any value". It is important to distinguish it from seemingly empty values, such as the empty string '' or the  number 0, neither of which are actually NULL. It is also important to be careful not to enclose NULL in quotes, like 'NULL', which is allowed in  columns that accept text, but is not NULL and can cause errors and incorrect data sets.)
/E (Chapter 39: NULL Introduction NULL in SQL, as well as programming in general, means literally "nothing". In SQL, it is easier to understand as "the absence of any value". It is important to distinguish it from seemingly empty values, such as the empty string '' or the  number 0, neither of which are actually NULL. It is also important to be careful not to enclose NULL in quotes, like 'NULL', which is allowed in  columns that accept text, but is not NULL and can cause errors and incorrect data sets.)
/Pg 148 0 R
/K [4]
>>
